WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:06.987
This is Allen Company training on the health side

00:06.987 --> 00:10.550
of the Mine Act. Part 48 requires that new miners

00:10.550 --> 00:12.875
learn the health provisions of the Act and how

00:12.875 --> 00:16.675
warning labels work. Safety is about today's injuries.

00:16.675 --> 00:20.088
Health is about what dust, noise, fumes and chemicals

00:20.088 --> 00:21.712
do to you over years.

00:22.849 --> 00:26.049
The Act directs MSHA to set health standards,

00:26.049 --> 00:29.498
like limits on dust, noise and diesel particulate,

00:29.498 --> 00:31.811
so that no miner suffers material impairment of

00:31.811 --> 00:35.061
health over a working lifetime. It requires monitoring

00:35.061 --> 00:37.724
of exposures, gives miners the right to see the

00:37.724 --> 00:40.474
results that apply to them, and requires labels

00:40.474 --> 00:42.924
and warnings so you know about the hazards.

00:44.071 --> 00:46.559
From time to time, you may wear a sampling pump

00:46.559 --> 00:48.959
or a noise dosimeter for a full shift.

00:48.959 --> 00:51.296
It measures what you're breathing and hearing.

00:51.296 --> 00:53.796
Wear it the whole shift, work the way you normally

00:53.796 --> 00:56.434
do, and never tamper with it. You'll be told the

00:56.434 --> 00:57.434
results.

00:58.610 --> 01:02.410
For noise, at an eight-hour average of 85 decibels,

01:02.410 --> 01:04.722
the mine enrolls you in a hearing conservation

01:04.722 --> 01:08.160
program, with audiograms and hearing protection.

01:08.160 --> 01:11.185
Above 90 decibels, controls are required and you

01:11.185 --> 01:13.097
must wear the protectors you're given.

01:13.097 --> 01:15.647
Noise damage is permanent and painless,

01:15.647 --> 01:17.772
so you won't notice until it's too late.

01:17.772 --> 01:20.310
At every Allen site, the loudest places are the

01:20.310 --> 01:23.085
plant and screens and the mine fans,

01:23.085 --> 01:25.510
and that's where hearing protection is required.

01:25.510 --> 01:27.560
Allen doesn't use double protection,

01:27.560 --> 01:30.047
plugs and muffs together, anywhere.

01:31.195 --> 01:34.295
For dust, our stone is very low in silica,

01:34.295 --> 01:37.057
but dust is still controlled with water sprays,

01:37.057 --> 01:40.082
enclosures and cab filters. Underground,

01:40.082 --> 01:43.232
diesel exhaust is limited by a DPM standard.

01:43.232 --> 01:46.657
There's a full DPM lesson in the Allen library.

01:47.772 --> 01:50.034
Warning labels and signs tell you about health

01:50.034 --> 01:53.047
hazards. Chemical labels use a signal word,

01:53.047 --> 01:56.234
pictograms, and hazard and precaution statements;

01:56.234 --> 01:58.635
the HazCom lesson covers them in detail.

01:58.635 --> 02:01.159
Area signs tell you where hearing protection or

02:01.159 --> 02:03.747
respirators are required. At Allen,

02:03.747 --> 02:06.260
respirators are required outside the screen plant

02:06.260 --> 02:08.547
while it's running, and at Boonesboro for certain

02:08.547 --> 02:11.597
shop and cleaning tasks. Danger means a hazard

02:11.597 --> 02:13.984
that will kill or seriously injure;

02:13.984 --> 02:16.747
warning and caution mean lesser hazards.

02:16.747 --> 02:20.522
Obey them, and report any that are missing or unreadable.

02:21.654 --> 02:24.042
You can report a health hazard to your supervisor

02:24.042 --> 02:27.454
or to MSHA, and the Act protects you from retaliation

02:27.454 --> 02:31.067
for doing so. Module 2 covers your rights in full.

02:32.184 --> 02:35.221
The Act sets health limits, requires monitoring,

02:35.221 --> 02:38.409
and requires warnings. Wear the sampler when asked.

02:38.409 --> 02:40.672
Protect your hearing and your lungs.

02:40.672 --> 02:43.147
Read and obey labels and signs.

02:44.274 --> 02:47.436
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

02:47.436 --> 02:48.937
override this video.
